-- title:
--   Residue ring of a π-integral extension is algebraic over ℤ/r
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a prime number, let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$ an element such that the quotient $\mathcal{O}/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (as a `Nat.card`). Let $O^{nr}$ be a commutative $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with the property that every $x \in O^{nr}$ satisfies a monic polynomial relation modulo $\pi$: there is a monic $p \in \mathcal{O}[X]$ with $p(x)$ lying in the ideal of $O^{nr}$ generated by the image of $\pi$. Assume moreover that the quotient ring $k_0 := O^{nr}/(\pi O^{nr})$ has characteristic $r$. Equipping $k_0$ with the $\mathbb{Z}/r$-algebra structure coming from the canonical ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}/r \to k_0$ (the `ZMod.castHom` attached to the characteristic), the conclusion is that $k_0$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/r$: every element of $k_0$ is a root of some non-zero polynomial with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}/r$. Note that no hypothesis forces $\mathcal{O}$ or $O^{nr}$ to be a domain, local, or $\pi$-adically complete, and $k_0$ is not asserted to be a field.
--
--   This is the elementary algebraic input behind the statement that the residue ring of an unramified (more precisely, $\pi$-integral) extension of a coefficient ring with residue field $\mathbb{F}_r$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{F}_r$. It is used in the construction of the supersingular base point in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where a fake elliptic curve with prescribed height-four formal module and full endomorphism dictionary is produced.
